WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.797
A token ID is only an index into the model’s vocabulary.

00:00:03.797 --> 00:00:07.542
The number itself does not contain a semantic geometry.

00:00:07.542 --> 00:00:12.477
Inside the model, that ID is used to look up a learned embedding vector.

00:00:12.477 --> 00:00:17.842
You can imagine a large table where each vocabulary item has a row of learned numbers.

00:00:17.842 --> 00:00:25.332
Position information is then incorporated so the model can distinguish the same token appearing in different

00:00:25.332 --> 00:00:30.007
locations. These initial vectors become the input to the first Transformer block.

00:00:30.007 --> 00:00:34.402
Attention and feed-forward layers repeatedly modify them.

00:00:34.402 --> 00:00:37.007
This gives us two useful terms.

00:00:37.007 --> 00:00:43.182
The initial embedding is the starting representation associated with the token and its position.

00:00:43.182 --> 00:00:51.027
A contextual hidden state is the updated representation after the token has interacted with the surrounding

00:00:51.027 --> 00:00:57.862
sequence. When we later call AutoModel and inspect last_hidden_state, we are looking at contextual vectors,

00:00:57.862 --> 00:00:59.918
not just the original embedding lookup.
